<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arthur Evans</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1851">1851</a>–<a href="/years/1941">1941</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sir Arthur Evans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Arthur Evans was a British archaeologist who excavated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> palace at Knossos on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> from 1900 and partly reconstructed it in concrete. He named the civilisation he uncovered 'Minoan' after the legendary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minos">King Minos</a>, devised its chronology and identified the scripts <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-a">Linear A</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/linear-b">Linear B</a>. He was keeper of the Ashmolean Museum in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arthur-evans.jpg" alt="Arthur Evans" width="320" height="437" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bassano Ltd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arthur_Evans_in_1939.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
